n Huang ; from gnews:84e90d3da2d3779da0e7bdb0 2026-07-14] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said he sees at least $1 trillion in revenue from the Blackwell and Rubin platforms through the end of calendar 2027.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, CEO Jensen Huang has said that he sees at least $1 trillion in revenue from Nvidia's Blackwell and Rubin platforms through the end of 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e281; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e438 [third_party_view; fool.com author Justin Pope; original source fool.com author Justin Pope 2026-07-14; from gnews:84e90d3da2d3779da0e7bdb0 2026-07-14] The author cites NVIDIA trailing 12-month revenue of $253 billion as the base for a forecast that sales could roughly double within two years.\\n    Quote: In other words, sales could roughly double within the next two years, based on Nvidia's trailing 12-month revenue of $253 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e24; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e439 [party_statement; The Motley Fool author, reporting TSMC's guidance; original source TSMC ; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] TSMC raised its forecast for global semiconductor-market revenue in 2030 from $1 trillion to $1.5 trillion, citing AI-fueled chip demand.\\n    Quote: Nvidia's foundry partner TSMC recently noted that the global semiconductor market's revenue could reach a whopping $1.5 trillion in 2030. The Taiwan-based foundry giant had previously anticipated $1 trillion in semiconductor revenue by the end of the decade. However, AI-fueled demand for chips led to a substantial upgrade to its guidance.\",\n   \"- e440 [third_party_view; The Motley Fool author, reporting TSMC's estimate; original source TSMC ; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] TSMC expects AI and high-performance-computing chips to represent 55% of the projected 2030 global semiconductor-market opportunity.\\n    Quote: TSMC points out that AI and high-performance computing (HPC) chips will account for 55% of this lucrative opportunity.\",\n   \"- e441 [fact; The Motley Fool author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] NVIDIA reported fiscal 2026 data-center revenue of $193.7 billion.\\n    Quote: For comparison, Nvidia's data center revenue in fiscal 2026 (which ended in January this year) was $193.7 billion.\",\n   \"- e442 [fact; The Motley Fool author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] NVIDIA's fiscal 2026 data-center revenue included $162.3 billion from compute-chip sales, with the remainder from networking components.\\n    Quote: It is worth noting that $162.3 billion of its fiscal 2026 data center revenue came from sales of compute chips, while the rest was from networking components.\",\n   \"- e443 [third_party_view; The Motley Fool author; original source Analysts ; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] Analysts believe NVIDIA's share of the AI data-center chip market may have peaked.\\n    Quote: However, analysts believe that Nvidia's AI data center chip market share may have peaked.\",\n   \"- e444 [third_party_view; The Motley Fool author, reporting analysts' estimate; original source Analysts ; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] Analysts anticipate NVIDIA's AI-chip market share will decline to 75% in 2026.\\n    Quote: That's why Nvidia's AI chip market share is anticipated to decline to 75% this year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e344; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e445 [third_party_view; The Motley Fool author; original source The Motley Fool author 2026-07-14; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] The author estimates that NVIDIA could generate more than $400 billion in 2030 data-center chip revenue if it has 50% market share of an $825 billion market.\\n    Quote: Let's assume Nvidia continues to lose ground in AI chips for the next four years and ends up at just 50% market share in 2030; it can still generate more than $400 billion in data center chip revenue in 2030 (based on the $825 billion market size estimated above).\",\n   \"- e446 [fact; The Motley Fool author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] NVIDIA reported fiscal 2026 networking revenue of $31.4 billion, up 142% year over year.\\n    Quote: The company reported a 142% year-over-year increase in networking revenue in fiscal 2026 to $31.4 billion.\",\n   \"- e447 [fact; The Motley Fool author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] NVIDIA reported fiscal 2027 first-quarter networking revenue of $14.8 billion, triple the year-earlier amount.\\n    Quote: It has started fiscal 2027 on a stronger note in this segment, with networking revenue tripling year-over-year to $14.8 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e272; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e448 [third_party_view; The Motley Fool author, reporting Mordor Intelligence's forecast; original source Mordor Intelligence ; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] Mordor Intelligence expects the InfiniBand market to grow 36% annually over the next five years and exceed $164 billion in revenue in 2031.\\n    Quote: The InfiniBand market, for instance, is expected to clock 36% annual growth over the next five years, according to Mordor Intelligence. It could generate more than $164 billion in revenue in 2031.\",\n   \"- e449 [third_party_view; The Motley Fool author, reporting Dell'Oro Group's forecast; original source Dell'Oro Group ; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] Dell'Oro Group projects that the data-center switch market will exceed $100 billion in revenue by 2030.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, the data center switch market is projected to exceed $100 billion in revenue by 2030, according to Dell'Oro Group.\",\n   \"- e450 [third_party_view; The Motley Fool author; original source The Motley Fool author 2026-07-14; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] The author estimates NVIDIA's combined data-center compute and networking addressable opportunity could exceed $1 trillion by 2030.\\n    Quote: In all, Nvidia's data center addressable opportunity, including both networking and compute, could surpass $1 trillion by the end of the decade.\",\n   \"- e451 [third_party_view; The Motley Fool author; original source The Motley Fool author 2026-07-14; from gnews:dadd84590540a466aea01b3c 2026-07-14] The author says consensus revenue-growth projections for NVIDIA through fiscal 2029 have increased significantly.\\n    Quote: That's why there has been a significant jump in Nvidia's consensus revenue growth projections through fiscal 2029.\",\n   \"- e453 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source TD Cowen analyst Joshua Buchalter 2026-07-08; from gnews:a0b0f251bbab383483ec679b 2026-07-14] TD Cowen analyst Joshua Buchalter said NVIDIA’s integrated hardware and software platform gives it an underappreciated competitive edge in the next AI phase, that AI spending is durable across applications and customers, and that industry-specific AI deployments will become a much larger growth driver over time.\\n    Quote: The analyst opines that NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA) possesses an underappreciated competitive edge in the next phase of AI. This is because it provides an integrated hardware and software platform, which works seamlessly together. The analyst remains confident that AI spending is durable throughout different applications and customers.\",\n   \"- e454 [party_statement; NVIDIA, in a company blog post by Andrew Burnes; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-14; from gnews:960a8db83f4da3c9133faea0 2026-07-14] NVIDIA said Gears of War: E-Day will launch with DLSS 4.5 on GeForce RTX systems on October 6, 2026.\\n    Quote: Launching October 6th, Gears of War: E-Day is further enhanced on GeForce RTX systems thanks to the day-one inclusion of DLSS 4.5.\",\n   \"- e455 [fact; David Jagielski, CPA; original source Broadcom ; from gnews:cf6b0ccecbe4028542c4e178 2026-07-14] Broadcom’s revenue growth was 48% in its most recent quarter, which ended May 3.\\n    Quote: Its growth rate was 48% in its most recent quarter (which ended May 3).\",\n   \"- e456 [party_statement; David Jagielski, CPA; original source Broadcom ; from gnews:cf6b0ccecbe4028542c4e178 2026-07-14] Broadcom said AI-related semiconductor revenue rose 143%, above its forecast.\\n    Quote: the company says that semiconductor revenue due to AI rose by 143%, coming in even higher than its forecast.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e408; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e457 [fact; David Jagielski, CPA; original source AMD ; from gnews:cf6b0ccecbe4028542c4e178 2026-07-14] AMD reported revenue of $10.3 billion, up 38% for the period ending March 28.\\n    Quote: For the period ending March 28, the company's revenue rose by 38% to $10.3 billion.\",\n   \"- e458 [fact; 247wallst.com, Vandita Jadeja; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1e1fa5a194198eaf05d90c9e 2026-07-14] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.615 billion, up 85.23% year over year.\\n    Quote: It delivered $81.615 billion in Q1 FY27 revenue, up 85.23% year over year\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e405, e477; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e459 [fact; 247wallst.com, Vandita Jadeja; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1e1fa5a194198eaf05d90c9e 2026-07-14] NVIDIA Data Center Networking revenue was $14.8 billion, up 199% year over year in Q1 FY2027.\\n    Quote: with Data Center Networking alone growing 199% YoY to $14.8 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e415, e479; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e461 [party_statement; 247wallst.com, Vandita Jadeja; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1e1fa5a194198eaf05d90c9e 2026-07-14] The author cited 214.5% year-over-year quarterly earnings growth and NVIDIA’s Q2 FY2027 revenue guidance of $91 billion.\\n    Quote: With quarterly earnings growth of 214.5% YoY and Q2 FY27 revenue guided to $91 billion, the 12-month models remain anchored to yesterday’s earnings power.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e274; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e462 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com, Vandita Jadeja; original source 247wallst.com, Vandita Jadeja 2026-07-14; from gnews:1e1fa5a194198eaf05d90c9e 2026-07-14] The author cited NVIDIA forward EPS of $8, a 25-times forward P/E, revenue growth above 85% year over year and gross margins of 75%.\\n    Quote: At $203.53 against forward EPS of $8, Nvidia’s forward P/E 25x is not expensive for a company compounding revenue north of 85% YoY with 75% gross margins.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e314; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e463 [third_party_view; The Motley Fool author, Harsh Chauhan; original source Third-party estimates for the 80% figure; Harsh Chauhan for the assessment that NVIDIA’s share is gradually slipping ; from gnews:37d2648cf990f4943cad6208 2026-07-14] Third-party estimates put NVIDIA’s share of the AI chip market at 80%, and the author says that share is gradually slipping.\\n    Quote: The good news is that Nvidia still controls an impressive 80% of the AI chip market, according to third-party estimates. However, its control over this lucrative market is gradually slipping.\",\n   \"- e464 [third_party_view; The Motley Fool author, Harsh Chauhan; original source Harsh Chauhan 2026-07-14; from gnews:37d2648cf990f4943cad6208 2026-07-14] The author says the shift from AI training to inference has increased demand for custom AI processors.\\n    Quote: This has led to increased demand for custom AI processors that are designed to perform specific tasks.\",\n   \"- e465 [third_party_view; The Motley Fool author, Harsh Chauhan; original source Harsh Chauhan 2026-07-14; from gnews:37d2648cf990f4943cad6208 2026-07-14] The author estimates Marvell controls 20% to 25% of the custom AI processor market.\\n    Quote: Marvell is one of the key players in custom AI processors, controlling 20% to 25% of this market.\",\n   \"- e466 [party_statement; The Motley Fool author, Harsh Chauhan; original source Marvell Technology ; from gnews:37d2648cf990f4943cad6208 2026-07-14] Marvell says it could pursue more than 50 custom AI chip opportunities across over 10 customers, with potential lifetime revenue of $75 billion.\\n    Quote: In all, Marvell notes that it can tap into more than 50 custom AI chip opportunities across over 10 customers in the long run, leading to potential lifetime revenue of $75 billion.\",\n   \"- e467 [rumour; Tom's Hardware, retelling a Financial Times report; original source NVIDIA, according to unnamed sources cited by the Financial Times ; from gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb 2026-07-14] NVIDIA reportedly cut its authorized-client roster in Asia by more than half and tightened checks, including data-center inspections, contract verification and end-user interviews, to reduce smuggling of its GPUs and servers into China.\\n    Quote: According to the Financial Times , this roster cuts the number of authorized clients by more than half, with those remaining having passed tougher compliance inspections to ensure that they are genuine businesses, not shell companies designed to forward Nvidia GPUs and servers into China. Some of the steps that Nvidia took to help safeguard its chips reportedly included sending staff to customer d\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e508, e510; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e468 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source Beijing ; from gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb 2026-07-14] China has refused to allow Chinese companies to buy NVIDIA H200 GPUs and is relying on domestic semiconductor manufacturers to fill the gap.\\n    Quote: However, Beijing refused to allow Chinese companies to buy these AI processors — instead, it’s banking on domestic semiconductor manufacturers to make up for the shortfall, but it’s apparently still not enough.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e223 (2026-07-08); both are in force: The Information reported on July 8, citing unnamed sources, that China was still determining the permitted H200 purchase quantity and the total could be fewer than 200,000 chips.\",\n   \"- e469 [rumour; Tom's Hardware, retelling a Financial Times report; original source An unnamed tech executive quoted by the Financial Times ; from gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb 2026-07-14] A tech executive told the Financial Times that Chinese domestic chip suppliers were sold out and that the executive's company was considering less powerful chips.\\n    Quote: One tech executive even told the Financial Times that all domestic suppliers are sold out and that they’re even considering less powerful chips, as long as they could be put to use.\",\n   \"- e470 [fact; 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ac8a489e54fbb1b85a1c7a2c 2026-07-14] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85.2% year over year.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA’s Q1 FY27 revenue hit $81.6 billion, up 85.2% YoY\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e246; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e471 [fact; 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ac8a489e54fbb1b85a1c7a2c 2026-07-14] NVIDIA Data Center Networking revenue grew 199% year over year in Q1 FY2027, attributed to InfiniBand, NVLink, and Spectrum-X pull-through.\\n    Quote: Data Center Networking alone growing 199% on InfiniBand, NVLink, and Spectrum-X pull-through.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e407; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e474 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois) 2026-07-14; from gnews:ac8a489e54fbb1b85a1c7a2c 2026-07-14] The author said NVIDIA traded at 22 times forward earnings with a 0.6 PEG ratio, alongside company guidance for $91 billion of Q2 revenue.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA trades at a forward P/E of 22x with a PEG of 0.6, unusual for a company guiding to $91 billion in Q2 revenue.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e417; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e475 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois) 2026-07-14; from gnews:ac8a489e54fbb1b85a1c7a2c 2026-07-14] The author judged NVIDIA compelling at 22 times forward earnings, citing 85% revenue growth and 75% gross margins, and characterized its mid-summer pullback as profit-taking.\\n    Quote: Paying 22x forward earnings for a business growing revenue 85% with 75% gross margins is rare, and the mid-summer pullback looks like profit-taking.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e33; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e476 [fact; Phoronix; original source System76 2026-07-14; from gnews:c6024d197f62679f8ff823a3 2026-07-14] System76 announced on 2026-07-14 that its new Adder Pro laptop would be offered with NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5060 or RTX 5070 graphics options.\\n    Quote: On the dedicated graphics side is the option for either the GeForce RTX 5060 or RTX 5070 graphics cards.\",\n   \"- e477 [fact; 247wallst.com (Vandita Jadeja); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:184645c8d683d95b684bd6da 2026-07-14] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 total revenue of $81.615 billion, up 85.23% year over year.\\n    Quote: Revenue hit $81.615 billion, up 85.23% YoY\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e458; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e478 [fact; 247wallst.com (Vandita Jadeja); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:184645c8d683d95b684bd6da 2026-07-14] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 Data Center revenue of $75.246 billion, up 92% year over year.\\n    Quote: Data Center alone contributing $75.246 billion (+92% YoY).\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e414; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e479 [fact; 247wallst.com (Vandita Jadeja); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:184645c8d683d95b684bd6da 2026-07-14] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 networking revenue of $14.8 billion, up 199% year over year.\\n    Quote: Networking was the sleeper hit at $14.8 billion (+199% YoY), driven by InfiniBand, Spectrum-X, and NVLink.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e459; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e480 [fact; 247wallst.com (Vandita Jadeja); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:184645c8d683d95b684bd6da 2026-07-14] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 non-GAAP EPS of $1.87, above expectations.\\n    Quote: Non-GAAP EPS of $1.87 beat expectations.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e270; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e481 [party_statement; 247wallst.com (Vandita Jadeja), quoting Jensen Huang; original source Jensen Huang, NVIDIA CEO ; from gnews:184645c8d683d95b684bd6da 2026-07-14] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said AI factory buildout was accelerating at extraordinary speed.\\n    Quote: “The buildout of AI factories , the largest infrastructure expansion in human history, is accelerating at extraordinary speed.”\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e472; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e482 [fact; 247wallst.com (Vandita Jadeja); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:184645c8d683d95b684bd6da 2026-07-14] NVIDIA had $119 billion in supply commitments; the item does not specify the commitments' period.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA is spending like a company that already won , with $119 billion in supply commitments\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e422; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e483 [party_statement; 247wallst.com (Vandita Jadeja); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:184645c8d683d95b684bd6da 2026-07-14] NVIDIA guided Q2 revenue to $91 billion, assuming no China Data Center compute revenue.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA guided Q2 to $91 billion in revenue, which assumes zero China Data Center compute.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e388; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e484 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com (Vandita Jadeja); original source 247wallst.com (Vandita Jadeja) 2026-07-14; from gnews:184645c8d683d95b684bd6da 2026-07-14] The author cited a 63% profit margin and 85% revenue growth for NVIDIA, describing the company as still compounding revenue at that rate.\\n    Quote: The $5.1 trillion market cap and 63% profit margin feel unusual for a company still compounding revenue at 85%\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e394; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e485 [fact; 247wallst.com, reporting figures attributed to NVIDIA; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:386a294bd395bde25e3f4a11 2026-07-14] NVIDIA reported Q1 FY2027 revenue of $81.61 billion, up 85.2% year over year, and Data Center revenue of $75.25 billion, up 92%.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA’s Q1 FY27 revenue hit $81.61B, up 85.2% YoY, with Data Center at $75.25B (+92%).\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e317; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e488 [fact; rdworldonline.com; original source Tecan ; from gnews:b46d410f1a3e3ad1888d51de 2026-07-14] Tecan integrated NVIDIA’s BioNeMo Agent Toolkit into its Introspect platform the day after NVIDIA released the toolkit in late June 2026.\\n    Quote: Tecan integrated the toolkit into Introspect the following day.\",\n   \"- e489 [third_party_view; Rest of World; original source Rest of World 2026-07-14; from gnews:01a49ffd41f6abe24538da65 2026-07-14] Saudi Arabia and the UAE have committed tens of billions of dollars to building AI industries.\\n    Quote: Saudi Arabia and the United Arab Emirates have committed tens of billions of dollars to becoming AI powers before the oil money fades.\",\n   \"- e490 [fact; Rest of World; original source Saudi Arabia 2026-06-01; from gnews:01a49ffd41f6abe24538da65 2026-07-14] Saudi Arabia agreed on June 1 to use self-driving taxis powered by Nvidia technology.\\n    Quote: Saudi Arabia agreed on June 1 to use self-driving taxis that run on Nvidia technology — from the computer inside each car to the software that steers it.\",\n   \"- e491 [fact; Rest of World; original source Humain ; from gnews:01a49ffd41f6abe24538da65 2026-07-14] Humain is building data centers in Riyadh and Dammam that are planned to use several hundred thousand Nvidia chips.\\n    Quote: Humain is also building data centers in Riyadh and Dammam, powered by several hundred thousand Nvidia chips.\",\n   \"- e492 [fact; Rest of World; original source G42 ; from gnews:01a49ffd41f6abe24538da65 2026-07-14] G42’s first stage of its Abu Dhabi Stargate data center is planned to run on 400,000 Nvidia chips.\\n    Quote: Its first stage will run on 400,000 Nvidia chips .\",\n   \"- e493 [party_statement; Rest of World; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:01a49ffd41f6abe24538da65 2026-07-14] Nvidia said its newest Blackwell computing architecture line was sold out.\\n    Quote: Its newest Blackwell computing architecture line is “sold out,” Nvidia said in the earnings report.\",\n   \"- e494 [third_party_view; Kamil Dimmich; original source Kamil Dimmich ; from gnews:01a49ffd41f6abe24538da65 2026-07-14] Kamil Dimmich said Qualcomm and Groq chips serve lower-cost inference tasks, while training powerful AI models still requires Nvidia chips.\\n    Quote: The Qualcomm and Groq chips are built to run finished AI models cheaply, for everyday tasks like answering a user’s questions, while training a powerful model still calls for Nvidia’s chips, Kamil Dimmich, a partner at emerging-markets investment firm North of South Capital, told Rest of World .\",\n   \"- e495 [third_party_view; Kamil Dimmich; original source Kamil Dimmich ; from gnews:01a49ffd41f6abe24538da65 2026-07-14] Kamil Dimmich said Humain’s first Nvidia order was for 18,000 Blackwell chips.\\n    Quote: Humain’s first order from Nvidia was 18,000 of its newest Blackwell chips — the hardware that does that heavy lifting, which no rival can yet match, he said.\",\n   \"- e496 [third_party_view; Kamil Dimmich; original source Kamil Dimmich ; from gnews:01a49ffd41f6abe24538da65 2026-07-14] Kamil Dimmich said high-speed memory and TSMC factory capacity are the two hardest inputs for chipmakers, including Nvidia, to secure.\\n    Quote: The two hardest things to secure are a specialized high-speed memory that keeps the chips fed with data, and space in the factories run by Taiwanese contract manufacturer TSMC, which makes chips for Nvidia and its rivals alike.\",\n   \"- e497 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source The U.S. government 2026-07-14; from gnews:3221eba2826b001b12b82770 2026-07-14] The U.S. approved ZTE and Maginfra to buy NVIDIA H200 chips; the item reports the approval on 2026-07-14.\\n    Quote: Today, Reuters reports that Chinese telecoms giant ZTE and server firm Maginfra have received U.S. approval to buy Nvidia's last-gen H200 \\\"Hopper\\\" chips.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e486; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e498 [third_party_view; Tom's Hardware, recounting Reuters; original source Reuters ; from gnews:3221eba2826b001b12b82770 2026-07-14] Reuters reported that Chinese technology firms had more than two million NVIDIA H200 chips on order about six months before 2026-07-14, exceeding NVIDIA's available supply at the time.\\n    Quote: But even with those domestic production initiatives, the Chinese hunger for AI silicon is so deep that six months ago, Reuters said the nation's tech firms had more than two million H200 chips on order, far more than what Nvidia had on hand at the time.\",\n   \"- e499 [party_statement; Tom's Hardware, quoting CNBC's account of a U.S. trade official's remarks; original source A U.S. trade official 2026-07-14; from gnews:3221eba2826b001b12b82770 2026-07-14] A U.S. trade official said that very few H200 and equivalent chips had shipped against licenses, describing the quantity as very small, on 2026-07-14.\\n    Quote: CNBC cites a U.S. trade official who today stated that \\\"very few shipments against licenses for H200s and equivalents have taken place. It’s a very small quantity of chips\\\" during a congressional hearing.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e430; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e503 [fact; blogs.windows.com; original source NVIDIA, XBOX and The Coalition 2026-07-14; from gnews:6ccac7bcb0b813cd4bf29c5d 2026-07-14] NVIDIA announced an exclusive PC partnership with XBOX and The Coalition for Gears of War: E-Day, including GeForce RTX integration, performance optimization and launch support.\\n    Quote: As the exclusive PC partner of this game, NVIDIA is collaborating closely with XBOX and game studio The Coalition to enhance the game’s PC experience through deep integration with GeForce RTX technologies, performance optimization and launch support.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e433; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e504 [fact; mlq.ai; original source FBI ; from gnews:ca7564a0dc63b215f5d49402 2026-07-14] The FBI published an RFI seeking market input on AI supercomputer hardware, including Nvidia Blackwell Ultra GPUs, for its CJIS Division; the RFI does not commit the FBI to a procurement.\\n    Quote: The FBI has published a Request for Information on SAM.gov seeking market input on AI supercomputer hardware for its Criminal Justice Information Services (CJIS) Division in Clarksburg, West Virginia. The RFI outlines four distinct categories of accelerator systems spanning training, inference, and analytics workloads — including both Nvidia Blackwell Ultra GPUs and Google TPUs [1] .\",\n   \"- e505 [third_party_view; mlq.ai; original source mlq.ai 2026-07-14; from gnews:ca7564a0dc63b215f5d49402 2026-07-14] Federal AI spending obligations reached $7.2 billion in 2026, up from $675 million in 2024.\\n    Quote: Federal AI spending obligations reached $7.2 billion in 2026, up from $675 million in 2024 — a 966% increase.\",\n   \"- e508 [rumour; Reuters; original source Financial Times, citing three people familiar with the matter 2026-07-14; from gnews:dc642adc9738cb72e71bf4cd 2026-07-14] The Financial Times reported on July 14, 2026, that NVIDIA more than halved its list of Asian customers authorised to buy its AI chips after establishing a new whitelist with tougher checks to prevent the chips reaching China.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA has more than halved the number of Asian customers authorised to buy its AI chips after creating a new “white list” of companies that have passed tougher compliance checks aimed at preventing the products from reaching China, the Financial Times reported on Monday (Jul 14).\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e467; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e509 [party_statement; Reuters; original source US Commerce Department ; from gnews:dc642adc9738cb72e71bf4cd 2026-07-14] The US Commerce Department issued guidance in May 2026 to curb advanced AI-chip shipments to overseas subsidiaries of Chinese companies and raised concern that NVIDIA Blackwell processors may have reached Chinese-linked entities in countries including Malaysia despite US restrictions.\\n    Quote: The US Commerce Department issued guidance in May aimed at curbing advanced AI chips from reaching overseas subsidiaries of Chinese companies, highlighting concerns that Nvidia’s cutting-edge Blackwell processors may have been exported to Chinese-linked entities in countries such as Malaysia despite US restrictions.\",\n   \"- e510 [rumour; Reuters; original source Financial Times, citing three people familiar with the matter 2026-07-14; from gnews:dc642adc9738cb72e71bf4cd 2026-07-14] The Financial Times reported that NVIDIA has tightened compliance checks, with staff visiting customers’ data centres, verifying contracts and interviewing end users.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has tightened compliance procedures following pressure from Washington, the FT said, adding that staff now visit customers’ data centres, verify contracts and interview end users as part of the checks.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e467; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\"\n  ]\n }\n]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
